 early  mixed martial arts was on the idea of  specialists in a given domain forcing  the fight into their domain  and that  uh my early work with George Saint  Pierre convinced me that the right  approach wasn't increased specialization  and learning to force  uh your athlete into that area of  specialization at the expense of the  opponent but rather the the real battles  of the future would be won and lost not  worth  techniques per se specialized techniques  but rather the integration of techniques  and the overlap between the various  grappling and striking skills  so that someone who was an inferior uh  uh Grappler would have just enough  grappling skills to be able to hold a  Grappler off and then defeat them with  uh striking and a striker who was uh if  you've went to fight a someone who's  Superior to you and striking you would  have just enough striking skill to be  able to hold them at Bay and then enter  into grappling this went further and  further until it got clear that there  were whole areas of the sport that  needed you need to change your entire  mindset about them  um  so that people went into  early MMA thinking in terms of Grappler  and Striker  um what I started to think is in terms  of okay there are four fundamental skill  areas of mixed martial arts there is  shoot boxing which is the integration of  uh  takedowns and and striking there is  clinch boxing which is the integration  of upper body clinch skills combined  with striking there is fence boxing  which the two athletes are locked up  with each other on the fence  um uh and they have to integrate take  down take down defense and striking  skills and there is grapple boxing which  is the merging of ground grappling with  striking and when you broke MMA down  into those four categories you saw that  each one of those four domains  transcends  the specialized martial arts that form  their components so for example in in  clinch boxing you would incorporate  things from Judo Greco-Roman freestyle  uh Jiu-Jitsu submissions Muay Thai  clinching techniques  but  even if you took all five of those  the rule set that you're operating in  required such extensive modification  that the final product of clinch boxing  transcended all five of its component  martial arts and became its own  autonomous skill needed to be worked  autonomously and when we broke  Georgia's training down into those four  areas that's when real progress started  to be made that's when you started to  see the integration of those four phases  and the Striking and grappling within  each of them  was where victory was being won and and  lost so once you reframe how you see a  particular combat sport  then you can start doing these detailed  development of ideas  that actually like they they fit there's  a sense in which that it had to start  with a paradigm shift and then a  research program began after that you  don't start with research you start with  the paradigm shift and then went to  research
 

